    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Iranian-speaking horse nomads who broke the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scythians">Scythians</a> and held the steppe from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volga">Volga</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> for six centuries.</li><li>Not one state but a confederation of tribes such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iazyges">Iazyges</a>, Roxolani, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aorsi">Aorsi</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siraces">Siraces</a> and Alans, each with its own kings.</li><li>Their armoured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lance">lancers</a> fought and served <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goths">Goths</a> and then the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huns">Huns</a> ended their dominance in the 3rd and 4th centuries.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a> claimed that a Sauromatian girl could not marry until she had killed an enemy in battle, and archaeologists have found many early Sarmatian women buried with weapons.</p></aside>
    </section>

