    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/herodotus"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/herodotus.jpg" alt="Herodotus" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Marble_bust_of_Herodotos_MET_DT11742_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">CC0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Sarmatians were an Iranian-speaking people of the Eurasian steppe, close in language and way of life to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scythians">Scythians</a> whom they displaced. Their forerunners were probably the Sauromatae, whom <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a> in the fifth century BCE placed east of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/don-river">River Don</a> (Tanais) and described as descendants of Scythian youths and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amazon">Amazons</a>. Archaeologists trace early Sarmatian culture, often called the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prokhorovka-culture">Prokhorovka culture</a>, to the steppes of the southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ural-mountains">Urals</a> and the lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volga">Volga</a> in the fourth century BCE. From the fourth and especially the third century BCE Sarmatian groups crossed the Don and pushed west across the Pontic steppe. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diodorus-siculus">Diodorus Siculus</a> records that they laid waste much of Scythia and destroyed its people, and by the second century BCE the surviving Scythian kingdom was confined to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crimea">Crimea</a> and the lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dnieper">Dnieper</a>. The Sarmatians never formed a single state. Ancient writers name a succession of tribal groupings, each with its own kings: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iazyges">Iazyges</a> and Roxolani, who led the advance towards the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a>; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aorsi">Aorsi</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siraces">Siraces</a> between the Don, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caucasus">Caucasus</a> and the Caspian; and, from the first century CE, the Alans, who rose to dominate the eastern steppe. ‘Sarmatians’ was therefore a broad name for a shifting confederation of related peoples rather than for one polity.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/black-sea"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/black-sea.jpg" alt="Black Sea" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Danube_Spills_into_the_Black_Sea.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">The SeaWiFS Project, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Most Sarmatians were pastoral nomads who moved with herds of horses, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> and sheep between seasonal pastures, living in felt tents and wagons, though some groups settled near the rivers and the Greek cities of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a> coast. Their wealth is known chiefly from burial mounds (<em>kurgans</em>), which contain weapons, horse harness, bronze mirrors, imported Greek and Roman vessels and jewellery in a polychrome style of gold set with turquoise, coral and glass, as in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/novocherkassk-treasure">Novocherkassk treasure</a> from the Khokhlach barrow on the lower Don. Clan and family property was marked with symbols known as <em>tamgas</em>. Trade linked the steppe with the Bosporan Kingdom, whose cities bought livestock, hides and slaves and sold wine, oil and craft goods, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strabo">Strabo</a> says that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aorsi">Aorsi</a> carried Indian and Babylonian goods on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/camel">camels</a>, received from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenians">Armenians</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medes">Medes</a>. Women held unusual prominence. A notable share of early graves belong to women buried with swords, arrows and armour, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a> claimed that a Sauromatian girl could not marry until she had killed an enemy, a tradition that fed Greek stories of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amazon">Amazons</a>. Rulers such as Queen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amage">Amage</a>, who according to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polyaenus">Polyaenus</a> led a raid against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scythians">Scythians</a> to protect the city of Chersonesus, show that women could also wield political power.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/marcomannic-wars"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/marcomannic-wars.jpg" alt="Marcomannic Wars" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/marcomannic-wars">Marcomannic Wars</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Column_of_Marcus_Aurelius_-_detail4.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Nico Kokkonen, CC BY 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Sarmatians were famous above all as horsemen. Their noble warriors fought as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heavy-cavalry">heavy cavalry</a>, protected by helmets and coats of scale or mail armour, and charged with a long lance, the <em>kontos</em>, held in both hands, while lighter riders used the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/composite-bow">composite bow</a>. This combination impressed Greek and Roman observers and influenced Roman cavalry, which later adopted armoured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lance">lancers</a> of its own. Sarmatian armies could be large: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strabo">Strabo</a> reports that Spadines, king of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aorsi">Aorsi</a>, could field 200,000 horsemen, a figure that is probably exaggerated. Their weaknesses were noted too. In 69 CE some 9,000 Roxolani raiders were caught by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/legio-iii-gallica">Legio III Gallica</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moesia">Moesia</a> on ground softened by a thaw, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tacitus">Tacitus</a> describes armoured riders unable to rise once unhorsed. Sarmatians fought both for and against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>'s enemies. The Roxolani under Tasius fought for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scythians">Scythians</a> against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diophantus-general">Diophantus</a>, general of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mithridates-vi-of-pontus">Mithridates VI of Pontus</a>, around 107 BCE; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iazyges">Iazyges</a>, who settled between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tisza">Tisza</a>, destroyed a Roman legion in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/domitian">Domitian</a>'s Sarmatian war of 92 CE; the Roxolani joined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/decebalus">Decebalus</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dacia">Dacia</a> against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trajan">Trajan</a>; and in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marcomannic-wars">Marcomannic Wars</a> the Iazyges fought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marcus-aurelius">Marcus Aurelius</a> until their king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zanticus">Zanticus</a> made peace in 175 CE. The Alans raided as far as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/media-region">Media</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a> in 72 CE and threatened Cappadocia in 135 CE.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/polybius"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/polybius.jpg" alt="Polybius" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/polybius">Polybius</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Stele_des_Polybios.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Jona Lendering, Livius Onderwijs, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Sarmatian government was tribal and personal. Each grouping was led by kings or chiefs drawn from leading clans, supported by an aristocracy of armoured warriors and a mass of herders who served as lighter cavalry; there was no capital, no written law and no permanent bureaucracy. Some kings were nonetheless powerful enough to act on the international stage. According to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polybius">Polybius</a>, the Sarmatian ruler Gatalos was included in the peace treaty that ended the war of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharnaces-i-of-pontus">Pharnaces I of Pontus</a> in 179 BCE, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tacitus">Tacitus</a> describes the rival kings Zorsines of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siraces">Siraces</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eunones">Eunones</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aorsi">Aorsi</a> taking opposite sides in the Bosporan war of 49 CE, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> backed Eunones. On the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a>, Rome managed its Sarmatian neighbours through treaties, hostages and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/subsidy">subsidies</a>. Hadrian settled a dispute over the payments made to the king of the Roxolani, and the peace imposed on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iazyges">Iazyges</a> in 175 CE limited where they could live and trade and required them to return Roman captives, reportedly some 100,000, and to supply 8,000 cavalry. In the fourth century Sarmatian society on the middle Danube was divided between a ruling group, the Argaragantes, and their subjects, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/limigantes">Limigantes</a>, who rose against their masters in 334 CE. Power thus rested on war leadership, kinship and the ability to extract gifts and tribute from settled neighbours.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/rome"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rome.jpg" alt="Rome" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Trevi_Fountain,_Rome,_Italy_2_-_May_2007.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Diliff, CC BY 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>For more than five centuries the Sarmatians dominated the steppe between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volga">Volga</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a>, and Greek and Roman geographers called the whole region <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sarmatia">Sarmatia</a>; Ptolemy's <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geography-ptolemy">Geography</a></em> divided it at the Don into European and Asian Sarmatia. Their <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heavy-cavalry">heavy cavalry</a> shaped warfare on the Roman frontier. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> recruited Sarmatian horsemen, most famously the 5,500 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iazyges">Iazyges</a> sent to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> in 175 CE, and an inscription from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ribchester">Ribchester</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lancashire">Lancashire</a> records a unit of Sarmatian cavalry still stationed there in the third century. Some scholars have argued that their traditions influenced the Arthurian legends, but this remains speculative. Sarmatian metalwork, with its inlaid gold and animal motifs, spread across the steppe and influenced the jewellery of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goths">Goths</a> and other peoples of the migration period. The Alans carried Sarmatian culture deepest into later history: one branch followed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vandals">Vandals</a> into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a>, where the Vandal kings styled themselves kings of the Vandals and Alans, while another remained in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caucasus">Caucasus</a>, where their descendants, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ossetians">Ossetians</a>, still speak an Iranian language derived from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alanic-language">Alanic</a>. In the sixteenth and seventeenth centuries the Polish nobility came to believe that it descended from the Sarmatians, an ideology known as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sarmatism">Sarmatism</a> that shaped its dress, portraiture and self-image.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/constantine-i"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/constantine-i.jpg" alt="Constantine I" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/constantine-i">Constantine I</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Statua_di_Costantino_ai_musei_capitolini.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Merulana, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Sarmatian dominance was undermined in the third century CE by the arrival of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germanic-peoples">Germanic peoples</a> from the north-west. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goths">Goths</a> reached the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a> region around the 230s, and by the later third century they controlled much of the Pontic steppe and forest-steppe, where the multi-ethnic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chernyakhov-culture">Chernyakhov culture</a> absorbed many Sarmatians. In the west, the Sarmatians of the Hungarian Plain became increasingly dependent on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantine-i">Constantine the Great</a> helped them against the Goths in 332 CE, and after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/limigantes">Limigantes</a> drove out their masters in 334 CE he admitted large numbers of Sarmatian refugees, reportedly 300,000, and settled them in the empire. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantius-ii">Constantius II</a> campaigned against the Sarmatians and Limigantes in 358–359 CE, installed a client king and took the title <em>Sarmaticus</em>. The final blow came from the east. In the 370s the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huns">Huns</a> crossed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volga">Volga</a>, defeated the Alans on the Don, absorbing many of them as allies, and then overran the Gothic realm of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ermanaric">Ermanaric</a> around 375 CE. The remaining Sarmatian groups were scattered: some followed the Huns, some fled into Roman territory as soldiers and settlers, and the Alans joined the great migrations of the early fifth century. Without the steppe as their base, the Sarmatians ceased to exist as a distinct people, merging into the populations of the late Roman world and the early medieval steppe.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Sarmatians, Iranian-speaking horse nomads who displaced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scythians">Scythians</a>, dominated the steppe from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volga">Volga</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> for some six centuries, fought and served <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> with their armoured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lance">lancers</a>, and passed their culture, through the Alans, to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ossetians">Ossetians</a> and their name to the self-image of early modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>.</em></p>

